name fab-envelope-ok-1
timeout 120

# THE FIRST STORE IN THIS TREE WHOSE CARRIAGE REFUSES A RELEASE. doc/air-to-ground.md §3.4.
#
# The FAB-500 M-62's documented release envelope is 500-1 000 km/h [T2, DCS-FM p.75], and the MiG-29
# reference base already rules it "a rejectable condition (out_of_context), not a guideline". In
# FlightBox that is CHECK 8 of FBStoresSystem::Release — after the two hardware interlocks and after
# the fire control's answer, because the envelope is a property of the STORE and not of the airframe
# and not of its computer. A catalogue row with no declared envelope is unaffected by construction,
# which is why every store written before this one behaves identically.
#
# THIS FILE presses the same pickle at 508 kt calibrated (941 km/h), inside the same envelope. The
# store leaves the pylon and nothing else about the run differs by a line.
#
# WHY AN F-16 CARRIES IT: the FAB rows are CARRIAGE AND BRIEFED RELEASE ONLY until C9 closes — the real
# MiG-29's unguided delivery is a DIRECTOR, not a release cue, so that module cannot fly `set task
# attack` at all. The store catalogue is this round's subject; the delivery mode is not.
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   events.log      `sms RELEASE station=3 store=fab500` followed by `sms RELEASE_SOLUTION`, and NO
#                   `sms RELEASE_ENVELOPE` line anywhere
#   telemetry.csv   sms_released goes 0 -> 1 and sms_loaded goes 1 -> 0
#
# READING RULE: exit 0 is not the measurement. The MEASUREMENT is the `sms RELEASE` line and the
# ABSENCE of a rejection. Read beside fab-envelope-reject.fbm — alone, this file proves nothing.

unit fitter
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.90000 6.80000 700 90.0 520
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 40
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off
  set store 3 fab500
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task route
  set brief_release_s 60
  wp 46.90000 7.40000 700 520
  objective survive
